{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity","id":1149444,"title":"System Complexity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-10-13T09:20:18-07:00","id":1149444,"title":"System Complexity","content":"I really like 2e, but one area where I feel it's taken a step backwards is with regards to complexity.<br /> <br /> As a Mage veteran I love the changes, but I've introduced 6 people to 2e that never played 1e and to say that the system is daunting would be an understatement.<br /> <br /> It would be nice to hear from other players about how they deal with this issue.<br /> <br /> One thing I've done:<br /> <br /> I've done away with basic free Reach and made all spells instant by default and allow +1 Reach for ritual magic. I don't understand why this was done the way it was. Players at the table expect instant spells and to explain that you're giving them a free Reach just to take it away for instant casting is confusing and pointless IMO.<br /> <br /> I use quick and dirty where possible, but it's really not that quick when you still have to calculate Reach.<br /> <br /> The fact that Reach changes ranges and advanced tables and then you need to figure out penalties to move within tables and then get Yantras into the mix gets a little complicated for almost all new players.<br /> <br /> I've never had a player click with the system yet even after 5 sessions in one 3 player group and 3 sessions in the other 3 player group.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T09:38:18-07:00","id":1149449,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Players at the table expect instant spells and to explain that you're giving them a free Reach just to take it away for instant casting is confusing and pointless IMO. </div> </div> </div>I suggested that was the case for most people not long ago in another thread and was talked out of the conversation by very offended Mage Fans. I expect them to either ignore this thread or jump to your throat until you realize oh how wrong you are and stop complaining since they don't see it as a problem. I hope I am wrong, but I don't have high hopes for polite conversation in the Mage part of the forums anymore.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T09:50:04-07:00","id":1149455,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m not sure you could create a system like this without such a level of complexity.<br /> <br /> I also am not sure that the complexity is any more of a problem than players who don\u2019t read the book to learn the rules which has been my experience with most games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T10:14:19-07:00","id":1149459,"title":"","content":"I can see why they phrased it the way they did, even though your version is effectively the same thing. &quot;Bonus reach&quot; isn't really a thing in universe, and I would hesitate to introduce things that suggest that it is for fear of setting a precedent. <br /> <br /> But if it helps your players to think of it that way, go for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T10:37:05-07:00","id":1149462,"title":"","content":"So... Awakening is my favorite CofD game and pretty much my favorite RPG. That said, I do concur that, while 2e has made the mechanics much more integrated with the setting -- and Reach is an effective way of stimulating the whole &quot;grasp for more power at your own risk&quot; thing -- it has made learning how to play much more complex and enabled more book-keeping.<br /> <br /> The complexity is only a problem in 2 situations: 1) players are too lazy to read the rules 2) you're trying to ST a one-shot for new players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Historian ~ <b><a href=\"http://www.nwodbrasil.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.cronistasdastrevasbr.com</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>I currently ST a... </b>MtAW 2e campaign called &quot;Axis Mundi - Si Vis Pacem&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/250-yuukale","name":"Yuukale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T10:39:55-07:00","id":1149464,"title":"","content":"It is also a thematic reinforcement. Even though you say player's 'expect' instant casting to be the default, that doesn't mean that that is the intended feel of the system. You are supposed to feel like Ritual is default and that casting with Instant makes you sacrifice other parts of the spell to do it. Mages are meant to be powerful when prepared and less so when caught by surprise, making ritual the default and paying for instant is part of this. It may not fit your groups playstyle if they want or 'expect' a more actiony game with it easier to fling fireballs around and it is fine to houserule it differently, but it isn't a mistake or pointless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T10:40:55-07:00","id":1149465,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity#post1149444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've done away with basic free Reach and made all spells instant by default and allow +1 Reach for ritual magic. I don't understand why this was done the way it was. Players at the table expect instant spells and to explain that you're giving them a free Reach just to take it away for instant casting is confusing and pointless IMO.</div> </div> </div> </div>While doing it this way around more readily fits the expactations of many player, its a backward way of doing things (in my opinion). It also would render the Time 4 attainment useless. It's however a valid way of explaining it to players who start the game, as its effectivly the same thing, just differently explained.<br /> <br /> But yeah mage is a very complex system and players who are not devouring the contents with obsessive fervor usually take quite a bit of time to get into the groove. But after a few sessions it usually starts getting better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T10:44:10-07:00","id":1149467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity#post1149444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The fact that Reach changes ranges and advanced tables and then you need to figure out penalties to move within tables and then get Yantras into the mix gets a little complicated for almost all new players.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>For this I would simply start without the advance tables with new players, that would help simplify it a bit without losing too much. You could just lock the ability to use advanced tables behind raising Gnosis to 2 or something if you want it more formal rather than just hand waved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":1329,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","user":"ElvesofZion","date":".  Fixed Quote block 10-13-2017, 01:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T10:54:00-07:00","id":1149470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flinty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149465#post1149465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It also would render the Time 4 attainment useless.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't make it useless. It changes it to &quot;the mage can spend one mana for a bonus reach on instant speed spells&quot; which, yeah, sounds a bit weird. <br /> <br /> Basically while everything is equivalent, the printed definitions are how it looks if you make everything go the same direction. You get so much free reach, and you can spend it to make spells more powerful or convenient. Voluntarily making them less convenient for bonus reach is functionally identical, but to actually phrase it that way in the book is, as you said. backwards. It goes against the grain and, in some cases, makes things seem to work in odd, counter-intuitive ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T12:59:38-07:00","id":1149501,"title":"","content":"Encouraging people to read the book has helped me in the past. There's plenty in the book that doesn't involve mechanics that is very engaging. I think that if you want people to be interested in the game then you want them to be interested in the roleplay and the themes and moods. The mechanics are there to enforce those ideas and to make it a playable game.<br /> <br /> Beyond that, I would say that playing a session or two as a sleeper before awakening would help some so that people are used to the general mechanics of the game (e.g. extended actions being difficult or easy depending on the skill and attribute involved, using d10s instead of d6s or d20s, social maneuvering, etc).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T14:18:11-07:00","id":1149539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity#post1149444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would be nice to hear from other players about how they deal with this issue.</div> </div> </div> </div>It hasn't really been an issue for me.<br /> <br /> The biggest deal for me getting people into Mage (any of the Mage games really, they're all daunting to new players that don't 'get it' immediately), is setting expectations. &quot;This game lets you make almost any spell you can imagine, so there's a lot of rules around building spells, that are a bit of a dense read and can seem pretty complicated. Do you have a preferred way to learn detailed systems?&quot; Has gotten me a long way since way back in trying to introduce people to Ascension 2e.<br /> <br /> If people have an expectation of instant casting if they've never played the game before... where is that coming from? When explaining the idea of the game, you should be setting the expectation that the book does: ritual casting is the default. It's not like the media inspirations for Mage are consistently &quot;instant casting is clearly the default.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't understand why this was done the way it was. </div> </div> </div>1) This actually reduces complexity from 1e. One of the biggest problems with the mechanics in 1e was ritual casting needing to be better than instant casting, without ritual casting becoming ridiculously broken. Every attempt to constrain ritual casting just made the system more and more complicated, but if you didn't it was super broken. Making ritual casting the default lets it be 'better' by having instant casting cost more to use (even if in a cheap resource).<br /> <br /> 2) The developers explicitly wanted to reduce the number of rolls for magic, and encourage smaller dice-pools for magic when mages are under duress. That's why the default is now one roll for rituals, and magic doesn't directly scale with number of successes rolled. If you want a Potency 10 damaging spell, you need to get your potency up to 10 by paying the price up front and hoping you can still get the success.<br /> <br /> 3) It pushes the thematic idea of mages reaching beyond their safe skills for more powerful magic. Grabbing a few extra Reach than you have for free to make a spell really powerful needs to be tempting, so there needs to be important things to spend it on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I use quick and dirty where possible, but it's really not that quick when you still have to calculate Reach. </div> </div> </div>There's a Reach chart in the back of the book. I'm not sure why this is particularly time consuming (free Reach = Rating in highest Arcanum used), and players don't have to spend it if they don't want to. If you have three free Reach and only want to spend two, stop with the two.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fact that Reach changes ranges and advanced tables and then you need to figure out penalties to move within tables and then get Yantras into the mix gets a little complicated for almost all new players. </div> </div> </div>Reach can be spent on Advanced spell factors setting them to a higher base level, though you still might want to purchase more on the better chart.<br /> Spell factors are purchased with penalty dice to your casting pool.<br /> Yantras is a catchall term for things that give you bonus dice to your casting pool. Some give you additional bonuses (such as reducing Paradox dice-pools).<br /> <br /> Once I've explained that... it all generally seems to go well. Yes, the game has a lot you can do with those concepts, but once the concepts are there, the complexity is pretty simple. &quot;I want to cast something that will last a year, so I use a Reach to give it Advanced Duration, take the dice penalties to get it to a year duration on the Advanced chart, and I use my Persona Yantra for +3 dice.&quot;<br /> <br /> But I always start new Mage players with, &quot;when you're going to cast a spell, always start with, 'what do I want the spell to do,'&quot; because I can always help walk people through how to get to that result with what they have on their sheet. Trying to pour through all the rules to figure out everything you can do tends to not end well, so don't have them start there. Once you're in the mindset of using the rules to adjudicate how to take an idea and then make it happen, instead of a tense text of what mages can and cannot do, the complexity issue tends to go down because you know you can skip 75% of the complexity for any given effort.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Thoughts? </div> </div> </div>House rules aren't the solution to this (esp. since they're not changing anything that's really an underlying issue).<br /> <br /> I think examining your approach to introducing people to the game might be more useful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T14:26:45-07:00","id":1149541,"title":"","content":"Huh. As someone who was turned off by the mechanical complexity of Mage 1e and finally won over by what I perceived to be the streamlining of 2e, this sentiment is very strange to me. Like, don't get me wrong, 2e is still very complex and will still be too intimidating for a lot of players. But in my experience it's more streamlined and approachable than 1e, not less.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-13T20:34:41-07:00","id":1149592,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149541#post1149541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh. As someone who was turned off by the mechanical complexity of Mage 1e and finally won over by what I perceived to be the streamlining of 2e, this sentiment is very strange to me. Like, don't get me wrong, 2e is still very complex and will still be too intimidating for a lot of players. But in my experience it's more streamlined and approachable than 1e, not less.</div> </div> </div> </div>There may be a similar sentiment to &quot;ChroD is more complicated than nWoD 1e because Tilts and Conditions exist&quot; in play here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T00:10:17-07:00","id":1149614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149541#post1149541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh. As someone who was turned off by the mechanical complexity of Mage 1e and finally won over by what I perceived to be the streamlining of 2e, this sentiment is very strange to me. Like, don't get me wrong, 2e is still very complex and will still be too intimidating for a lot of players. But in my experience it's more streamlined and approachable than 1e, not less.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if it's right to say spellcrafting in one edition or another is more or less complex, but what Mage 2E's system has different is that it's more <i>involved.</i> Now instead of just determining factors and firing to see how effective your spell is (which varied to annoying degrees even as a Master sometimes), now you craft the whole thing from the ground up, weighing the risks of how far down you reduce your dice pool, taking into account Reach and therefore Paradox and how much you're capable of reducing that.<br /> <br /> There are more considerations that go into spellcasting than there were in 1E. It was both difficult to wrap my head around it, and it's been the trend to the friends I've introduced it to; spellcasting is always the thing they take the longest time internalizing. Worse, attempting to remove or significantly change parts of it for simplicity's sake can often de-emphasize certain important elements to spellcasting which define Mage's themes and aesthetics.<br /> <br /> The free Reach you get when casting at a spell's level is a good sort of all this; it exists so that a player can elect to cast a spell instantly (since what's even the goddamn point of being able to cast Magic Missile if it takes three hours to go off?) or to instead augment a spell for broader purposes through ritual casting. The ability to sacrifice instant casting to be able to achieve a more useful effect with prep time is a good introduction to the idea of Ritual Casting and its usefulness--but the natural assumption, since a given session tends to take place in the immediate with the need for immediate action instead of over extended periods, people tend to think of immediate casting as the default, and breaking them of that very natural assumption is vital to evoking the ritualistic and symbolic trappings that make up a massive chunk of Mage's aesthetics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come visit our unofficial Mage: the Awakening Discord Server!<br /> <a href=\"https://discord.gg/Z42DJ9h\" target=\"_blank\">https://discord.gg/Z42DJ9h</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13241-biston","name":"Biston","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T02:34:01-07:00","id":1149626,"title":"","content":"Maybe I'm weird, but I kind of picture it like a control panel. You got switches and sliders. The sliders all have two scales up the sides, except potency, and the switches switch between which one they use. Flicking a switch costs a reach, pushing a slider up costs 2 dice a notch. There's also a couple of switches that toggle casting speed and range and stuff.<br /> <br /> And from there you just sort of arrange it until the spell does what you want.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T03:33:48-07:00","id":1149633,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The free Reach you get when casting at a spell's level is a good sort of all this; it exists so that a player can elect to cast a spell instantly (since what's even the goddamn point of being able to cast Magic Missile if it takes three hours to go off?) </div> </div> </div>I wonder where was people like you or the OP when I was aggresively said that was not the case. By the way, even if you didn't get that Free Reach for Instant Casting, you still could do spells on the fly, the only limit Reach really has is how much Paradox you are ready to deal with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T06:17:07-07:00","id":1149662,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149449#post1149449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I suggested that was the case for most people not long ago in another thread and was talked out of the conversation by very offended Mage Fans. I expect them to either ignore this thread or jump to your throat until you realize oh how wrong you are and stop complaining since they don't see it as a problem. I hope I am wrong, but I don't have high hopes for polite conversation in the Mage part of the forums anymore.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This post made my day, love you forumposter<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity#post1149444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>The way i learn Mage and also the way i run it is as such.<br /> <br /> First i assume player wont ever read the manual and accommodate to that assumption. Players sit on the table and expect a game to happen with a minimal effort on their part. Are there people who do read it? Sure but those people arent a problem. Second assumption, all players are looking to &quot;win&quot;, by this i mean they always looking for the maximum efficiency (to whatever capacity they have) in everything they do, so things like &quot;not using all their reach&quot; wont fly. If i suggest that they will reply &quot;no no no wait wait, just let me think&quot; and grind the game to a halt while they are looking for that one use of free reach and insisting to let it go just to continue the game results in sours looks and resentment.<br /> <br /> So on how i do it:<br /> PCs start with no order and a cap on an arcanum at 2. Then they cant spend XP on raising Arcanums until a time skip happens (about 3 month of actual play) and then they can raise it to a 3. And so on.<br /> <br /> Why i do this? Because mage (all mage lines) spoils you with choices to the point that to that kind of player is paralyzing. So by starting in arcanum 2 i cut down the choices available to the PCs, they get less things to worry about and less capabilities. And the 3 months of play with trapped in a level of arcanum lets them get acclimate to both the system and the arcanum.<br /> <br /> When i started with ascension i read the manual and had knowledge of the mechanics enough but being trapped in sphere 2, made really internalize the rules. And the rest of the table who never ever touched the manual were able to play without ever reading. By rote (lol) they learned what they needed to do to get the spells they wanted. It gave me a better appreciation of each level of spheres with some spells being actual improved versions of their 2 sphere 2 versions. The progressions felt both more natural and earned than the basic assumption of starting.<br /> <br /> As DM, this also allows me to regulate the power level of the game. Managing a semblance of street level while building up to more big stuff over time. It also allows some credibility to the setting by avoiding 19 year old masters but thats a matter of personal taste.<br /> <br /> So thats my advice, but beyond reframing the way you present the game/rules there is little you can do to fix your issue without houseruling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T08:14:50-07:00","id":1149687,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Biston</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149614#post1149614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know if it's right to say spellcrafting in one edition or another is more or less complex, but what Mage 2E's system has different is that it's more <i>involved.</i> Now instead of just determining factors and firing to see how effective your spell is (which varied to annoying degrees even as a Master sometimes), now you craft the whole thing from the ground up, weighing the risks of how far down you reduce your dice pool, taking into account Reach and therefore Paradox and how much you're capable of reducing that.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, I love that. Now I'm in control of exactly what my spell does, and the only limiting factor is how many dice and Reach I'm willing to risk. It's much easier for me to start from what exactly I want the spell to do, and then work backwards to figure out how many dice and Reach I need to make it happen. Plus the spell factor tables are so much more streamlined now. No more separate factor tables for instant cast vs. ritual cast, all the various scale factor tables got consolodated into a single table, and everything is always -2 dice to go up a step. Much easier for me personally to grasp.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Biston</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149614#post1149614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are more considerations that go into spellcasting than there were in 1E. It was both difficult to wrap my head around it, and it's been the trend to the friends I've introduced it to; spellcasting is always the thing they take the longest time internalizing. Worse, attempting to remove or significantly change parts of it for simplicity's sake can often de-emphasize certain important elements to spellcasting which define Mage's themes and aesthetics.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, spellcasting was what I couldn't wrap my head around in 1e. I got that in theory you could do pretty much anything with the right Arcanum, but it was really hard for me to figure out what Arcanum were required for what effects, and then on top of that you had to weigh the dice penalties for your spell's factors against your chances of getting enough successes on the roll to do what you wanted. In 2e all the weird corner cases with Arcanum got lumped into Reach effects, and the probability math is so much easier when all spells are just pass/fail. No need to worry about how many dice you'll need to reliably roll X successes, because you only ever need 1 success.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Biston</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149614#post1149614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The free Reach you get when casting at a spell's level is a good sort of all this; it exists so that a player can elect to cast a spell instantly (since what's even the goddamn point of being able to cast Magic Missile if it takes three hours to go off?) or to instead augment a spell for broader purposes through ritual casting. The ability to sacrifice instant casting to be able to achieve a more useful effect with prep time is a good introduction to the idea of Ritual Casting and its usefulness--but the natural assumption, since a given session tends to take place in the immediate with the need for immediate action instead of over extended periods, people tend to think of immediate casting as the default, and breaking them of that very natural assumption is vital to evoking the ritualistic and symbolic trappings that make up a massive chunk of Mage's aesthetics.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, to me the nice thing about ritual casting in 2e is that it doesn't much matter if you think about it as ritual being the default and instant costing you 1 reach or if you think about it as instant being the default and ritual giving you +1 free reach. It's functionally the same, so which ever is easier for you to grok, just treat it that way. More importantly, the spellcasting mechanics are exactly the same whether you are ritual casting or instant casting. This goes such a long way towards making the system more approachable for me because I don't have to think about two different ways to produce every effect and figure out which is going to be more effective. No more calculating dive penalties on an Instabt action vs. target number of successes on an extended action and having to do even more probability math to figure out which is the better one to use. A spell is always just a spell, the same effect is produced the same way whether you need it right now or have the luxury of taking your time. The only difference is, if you can't take your time, you need 1 more Reach than if you can. Simple.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T09:07:00-07:00","id":1149705,"title":"","content":"I genuinely think its something of a toss up as to whether 1E or 2E have a more complicated system. I will happily argue that 2E's is better, more consistent, and fun to manipulate once you master it though. Its attaining that system mastery that can be the problem.<br /> <br /> People are free (and correct) to throw criticisms of anecdotal evidence at me, but here's what I have picked up from my current game. I am currently running for 5 players. 2 have played a little 1e, the others are new to NWOD entirely. Both of my old hands have adapted pretty rapidly, but I had to be on the ball a lot to point out misconceptions and mistakes (lots of cases where key words have survivied the edition change, but actually mean different things, such as Potency not being linked to successes, not rolling against Withstood effects etc).<br /> <br /> For the sake of the new players I made a point in the first 3 sessions to model how we were putting spells together whenever the more savvy guys did it, verbally working through how much free reach was in play, how it was being spent etc. When the new players cast their spells (none of whom had done more than glance at the spellcasting section), I always had them suggest what effect they wanted to pull off, then offered a suggestion on how reach/dice pools/yantra's could be arranged, (this could be used to showcase any options they might not have been aware existed IE&quot;For one more reach you can target all 5 of them!&quot;). After showing them how I was doing this, it wasn't long at all before they were making their own suggestions, and then finally just taking back control of how the spells were constructed.<br /> <br /> So something like 10 plus sessions in, 4 players happily slap there own effects together, 1 is a bit newer (we had one drop, another come in) and is still at the 'here's how I would do it' stage, but I have high hopes that won't last much longer.<br /> <br /> A couple of things I would recommend.<ul><li>Print out the quick spellcasting chart from the back of the book. Laminate it. Slap it in the middle of the table at the start of the session.</li> <li>If you have players who don't have the time/inclination to the read the book, heavily suggest they invest in a NPC Mentor and come up with a concept that involves Awakening recently. Then you can turn learning about the setting (which can be just as much of a complexity barrier as the rules) can be made into a fun role-play experience, and the Mentor can point out magical options/tactics that the player might not know was available to them.</li> <li>Make the Mundane relevant. Tied in with the complex magic rules, is a pretty simple and flexible core dice rolling system. Try and ensure that mundane rolls occur, and that mundane strategies and approaches have a reasonable chance of success. This doesn't necessarily help deal with the complex casting factor, but it helps ensure less confident players still have a strong sense of agency, keeping them invested in your game long enough for them to learn by osmosis.</li> <li>Encourage creative thaumaturgy: Welcome players throwing out their idea's for effects they want, then work backwards with them to achieve it or something similar.</li> <li>Don't throw too many antogonistic Adepts at your new players, but it is worth having the occasional NPC Mage pull out some weird, dramatic effect on them. Players will want to work out how to do it themselves! I nearly drowned my players last session when the necromancer they were hunting transmuted every solid object in the house they were investigating (apart from the walls and roof) into a liquid. The looks on peoples faces when he *dismissed* the spell on the next turn was priceless. My Moros player is now much more excited about the possibilities of State Change.</li> <li>Love and praise those guys who actually read the book. Use them shamelessly to assist other players in hectic situations like combat, where your focus is being spent on NPC's actions and who-evers turn it currently is.</li> </ul>Thats my 2 cents, anyway!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6230,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","user":"Katana1515","date":". 10-14-2017, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","name":"Katana1515","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T09:24:38-07:00","id":1149707,"title":"","content":"Yeah, which system is easier to learn will depend a lot on the individual. 2e's spellcasting clicked for me where 1e's confounded me, but it makes sense some people would have the opposite experience.<br /> <br /> Personally, I wouldn't want to run mage for anyone who hasn't at least read the player-facing rules. There are systems that are easy enough to pick up with just your imagination and a rules-savvy GM, but Mage is not one of them. It can be done, but it'd be a real challenge, and I generally expect my players to read the rules that are relavent to them regardless of system unless they're new to RPGs, and Mage would never be the game I'd use to introduce people to RPGs for the first time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T09:58:39-07:00","id":1149717,"title":"","content":"I guess one thing you could do is go for down and dirty combat magic. Just roll gnosis + arcarnum, does [success] damage. Assume free reach only and call it bashing at 3 dots, lethal at 4, agg at 5 for a mana. If they want to push it further, hey, that's what the <i>real</i> spellcasting rules are for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T11:38:26-07:00","id":1149750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149717#post1149717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess one thing you could do is go for down and dirty combat magic. Just roll gnosis + arcarnum, does [success] damage. Assume free reach only and call it bashing at 3 dots, lethal at 4, agg at 5 for a mana. If they want to push it further, hey, that's what the <i>real</i> spellcasting rules are for.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Wouldn't that would feel like a bit of a trap option to offer new players? Since the standard 'Attack' spell each Arcanum grants at 3 dots will almost certainly provide better results, assuming the PC is a Disciple at least. I suppose you could add a flat damage boost to it, but not sure exactly what number. Arcanum - 1?<br /> <br /> I am a little leery of letting people rely on Down and Dirty too much anyway. Its great for on the fly narrative effects, subtle little tricks/pranks and for situations where not a lot is at stake and both GM and Players just want to move on to the next scene, but I wouldn't want to encourage people to rely on it in combat or major scenes. Feels like it might just become a crutch to avoid learning the system at all.<br /> <br /> Thats just my gut reaction though, different tables might respond differently.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","name":"Katana1515","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T11:53:22-07:00","id":1149754,"title":"","content":"Hmm, thinking about it it might be on the weak side...<br /> <br /> 'Course, to figure that out you have to, y'know, <i>learn the system</i> so you might get away with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T11:54:22-07:00","id":1149755,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149633#post1149633\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I wonder where was people like you or the OP when I was aggresively said that was not the case. By the way, even if you didn't get that Free Reach for Instant Casting, you still could do spells on the fly, the only limit Reach really has is how much Paradox you are ready to deal with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but forcing players to eat Paradox every time they try to use their new powers in the most basic and likely fashion they're going to want to and need to exercise them isn't that fun. You also end up with new characters either chewing through their mana pool or just not casting at all--something that feels like it jives against the &quot;carrot over stick&quot; philosophy that's been cited before, and quickly results in newly-created characters not using the powers that the system encourages you to abuse (and reap the consequences of).<br /> <br /> The OP's system is a pretty good one as far as making it easier to understand for new players, I think, as it functions mechanically identical but the explanation is based on the sort of natural assumptions someone new to the game would make.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come visit our unofficial Mage: the Awakening Discord Server!<br /> <a href=\"https://discord.gg/Z42DJ9h\" target=\"_blank\">https://discord.gg/Z42DJ9h</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13241-biston","name":"Biston","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T17:06:43-07:00","id":1149799,"title":"","content":"Once the basics were explained, the defaults known, the tables handy, notes about what yantras they usually have etc it was quite easy for my players to just compile dice pools and remove 2 dice per spell factor and then work out what they used it on. Something easy about having 10 dice in front of you and removing 8 and then deciding where those 4 steps went. They never really had a problem with reach, once they knew how much they have for free it wasnt a problem to decide where to put it and to then decide if they need to over reach.<br /> <br /> They were 1e players but i think that actually hindered them, it took them awhile to remember that sensory range is not 200 yards anymore, or little things like mage armor not being a spell, only needing 1 success on a spell that sort of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T17:38:31-07:00","id":1149806,"title":"","content":"I'm surprised Mage Sight hasn't been touched upon in this thread. I find it a very interesting mechanic that integrates perfectly with the whole idea of supernatural detectives but, for starting players or a one-shot in a convention, this seems to just add to a pile of rules.<br /> <br /> Granted, starting players on a long-term chronicle is an entirely easier problem to solve (presenting one thing at a time, say: one session you teach through play the rules for improvised spells, the next session you tackle mage sight, the next one covers a different aspect and so on...).<br /> <br /> My personal solution for con-games is to just print a truckload of reference sheets. One with condensed PMS, AMS, FMS rules, another with spell factors, and a third group of sheets with all the copy-pasted rotes the pre-gen characters have access to (cons aren't build-your-cofd-character friendly - Mage seems to take the most time of all the gamelines).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Historian ~ <b><a href=\"http://www.nwodbrasil.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.cronistasdastrevasbr.com</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>I currently ST a... </b>MtAW 2e campaign called &quot;Axis Mundi - Si Vis Pacem&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/250-yuukale","name":"Yuukale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T17:43:32-07:00","id":1149809,"title":"","content":"Mage sight is actually an interesting mention. In my experience players complain that you can get more information easier and less costly by just using unveiling or knowing spells and they aren't necessarily wrong. Mage sight sometimes (not all the times) seems uneccesarily bothersome compared to simple spells.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-14T21:38:35-07:00","id":1149851,"title":"","content":"I think spells are actually supposed to be easier to use than Mage Sight for figuring out relevant details of a Mystery. What Mage Sight does is, when you have reached Opacity 0 and made that last Revelation roll, guarantee that you have plumbed all possible information out of a Mystery. In other words, with spells, you can be fairly sure you know what you wanted to find out. With Mage Sight, you can be sure there's nothing left to learn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T00:09:44-07:00","id":1149865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flinty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149809#post1149809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mage sight is actually an interesting mention. In my experience players complain that you can get more information easier and less costly by just using unveiling or knowing spells and they aren't necessarily wrong. Mage sight sometimes (not all the times) seems uneccesarily bothersome compared to simple spells.</div> </div> </div> </div>My players still break out Focused Mage Sight quite regularly. I usually set Opacity levels pretty low (3 max) unless its a major plot critical mystery. They like that I always grant them Arcane beats for hitting Opacity 0, and I make a point of giving them quite a bit of information at each level of Opacity they breach. I usually write these up before the session, and they are a great way to hand out answers/clues that the PC's didn't even know they wanted yet. <br /> <br /> Basically in a large/varied Cabal someone can probably create a Knowing/Unveiling to answer any specific question that comes into the players heads, but FMS can tell them things they wouldnt have even thought to ask for. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","name":"Katana1515","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T02:01:30-07:00","id":1149880,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yuukale</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1149806#post1149806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm surprised Mage Sight hasn't been touched upon in this thread. I find it a very interesting mechanic that integrates perfectly with the whole idea of supernatural detectives but, for starting players or a one-shot in a convention, this seems to just add to a pile of rules.<br /> <br /> Granted, starting players on a long-term chronicle is an entirely easier problem to solve (presenting one thing at a time, say: one session you teach through play the rules for improvised spells, the next session you tackle mage sight, the next one covers a different aspect and so on...).<br /> <br /> My personal solution for con-games is to just print a truckload of reference sheets. One with condensed PMS, AMS, FMS rules, another with spell factors, and a third group of sheets with all the copy-pasted rotes the pre-gen characters have access to (cons aren't build-your-cofd-character friendly - Mage seems to take the most time of all the gamelines).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is actually a good point, and is actually what i as a St struggle the most with in 2e, trying to describe things seen under mage sight without refering to a &quot;acid trip&quot; every time and also making up mysteries opacity etc and coming up with bits of info for each layer. It was also a minor gripe my players had in 1e (the whole mage sight spells, and system in general) and they seem to go to great lenths to not bother using it eve going so far as to (try) getting npc's to do it for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 10-15-2017, 05:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-16T04:37:45-07:00","id":1150115,"title":"","content":"My players found spellcasting a lot easier in 2ed than 1ed, everything is more streamlined.<br /> Making every spell instant but no free reach, is a nice hack.<br /> <br /> Mage Sight might be bothersome, put it\u00b4s rewarding imho. I pointed out the difference by these simple sentences:<br /> - Mage Sight - give you answers for questions you did not ask.<br /> - Knowing/Unweiling - gives you an answer (singular) for what you asked.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Gamelines: Requiem, Lost, Awakening, Trinity Continuum: Aeon, some V5.<br /> Likes cheesecake (quark-based)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1305-maekkel","name":"maekkel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1305_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"206"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T07:01:55-07:00","id":1150334,"title":"","content":"First of all, those of you with super involved players - congratulations.<br /> <br /> My experience in the D&amp;D world we live in:<br /> <br /> 1. Players won't read the book. You'll be lucky if they read their Order and Path sections.<br /> 2. Players do expect Instant spells as their frame of reference is D&amp;D and Doctor Strange.<br /> 3. 2e IS more complex than 1e for instant spells. Not to mention that things that used to be spells like Mage Sight and Mage Armor are now attainments.<br /> <br /> In my last Chronicle I found the players were very good sports and were really trying to get a handle on the system, but after explaining Reach, Spell Factors, Yantras etc... and then explaining the difference between Spirits, Ghosts, Goetia and then moving on to the new Mage Sight rules their heads were exploding. They almost cried when they found out the answer to the question: &quot;What's this Legacy thing on my sheet?&quot;<br /> <br /> Anyway, there's been some useful suggestions in this threads, so thanks to those who acknowledge the issue.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T07:42:47-07:00","id":1150348,"title":"","content":"You can basically boil down spell factors into 5 questions: How Fast? How Far? How Strong? How Long? and How Big?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T10:30:29-07:00","id":1150392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150334#post1150334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> They almost cried when they found out the answer to the question: &quot;What's this Legacy thing on my sheet?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, as a fan of Mage I love Legacies as a concept and definitely think they add a lot to the gameline. However I too found they can be the straw that breaks the camels back with new players. I am fairly sympathetic about this, your average player is already trying to deal with the pretty hefty and specific symbolism and theory that comes with choosing a Path and a Order, coming up with a unique Obsessions and Aspirations, as well as doing the important work of making a character concept that excites them and feels real and human. Trying to add a discussion about Legacies onto that seems like playing with fire, I have let the subject lie pretty fallow with my lot (apart from one of my old hands, who is desperate to join the Blank Badges), and instead intend to &quot;Show not tell&quot; by incorporating some legacy themed plotlines in my next few arcs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","name":"Katana1515","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T10:45:58-07:00","id":1150394,"title":"","content":"Well as far as legacies go....<br /> <br /> Most PCs don't start out with them, so you can usually just slowly introduce them into the story when you feel the characters are ready for them. By the time they want to join legacies they'll have seen a bunch of NPCs who were subtly showing off their legacy and a few who did so not so subtly (such as one NPC from my game who took the formal dress code of an important consilium meeting as an opportunity to show off the traditional garb of his legacy - walking into the room with a long coat and sunglasses while everyone else was sporting robes and mystical designs <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ).<br /> <br /> There are also mentor figures showing off what they can accomplish with their legacies, etc. By now out of four players (and rouighly ~ 2.5 stories into the campaign) three of the four are considering taking up legacies, two of them at least seemed pretty excited by the prospect. The fourth player who has the least planned in that direction is ironically the only player who actually was familiar to the game prior to this campaign.<br /> <br /> So all together legacies are in my experience not too much of a problem. You don't need to deal with it in the beginning and interest on the playerside will occur naturally when you show them off doing cool things via NPCs. The only problem I see is that legacies frankly aren't very capable of doing cool things you couldn't just have done with an improvised spells RAW, but that's probably not a discussion for this thread.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T10:49:16-07:00","id":1150395,"title":"","content":"Yeah, legacies are more limited than spells. That's the payoff for the &quot;no paradox ever&quot; thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T11:55:56-07:00","id":1150404,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150395#post1150395\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, legacies are more limited than spells. That's the payoff for the &quot;no paradox ever&quot; thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well since you're limited to free reaches there wouldn't be any paradox to begin with. Unless you're counting sleeper witnesses, but there the lack of quiescence is arguably more important. My gripe is less with the lack of flexibility of legacies and more with the fact that they don't give you a thing you're especially good at. I'd like legacies to buck the system a bit, enable stuff you couldn't usually just pull off with a spell. You can see some examples of that in my homebrew thread. As it is I've seen a few players go at legacies with the attitude of &quot;well lets get one level in here for the extra ruling arcanum and the free hallow, I don't really care about the attainments.&quot; Sure that's just a player ignoring rich fluff, but there should be some substance to the fluff aswell. Mages shape their very soul to adopt a legacy, they don't do that just for some extra Mana, they do it to dedicate themselves to a particular kind of mystery they could never explore in this depth otherwise!<br /> <br /> That said I'll stop posting about this here now as it's not the topic of this thread, maybe I'll open a thread of its own concerning legacies and how they might be improved upon in time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T12:33:03-07:00","id":1150415,"title":"","content":"Legacy attainments don't take up spell control is the main draw for me. This allows you to maintain a set of buffs persistently without draining spell control, or keep multiple instances of an attainment running simultaneously on different subjects.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T13:08:53-07:00","id":1150431,"title":"","content":"Yeah. I generally paper over Legacies for at least a few stories now. Best to leave it tucked away until people understand the system better.<br /> <br /> I'm trying to put together some hand outs that will help with system complexity issues. I'll let you guys know how it goes.<br /> <br /> The reality is that those &quot;joke&quot; flowcharts regarding Awakened magic that are floating around out there aren't actually wrong about how complex the decision tree is for casting a spell in this game. I'm going to try to boil it down to the essentials and go from there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T13:35:43-07:00","id":1150445,"title":"","content":"How often do you use down n dirty casting? Is it something you use alot?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T13:45:56-07:00","id":1150452,"title":"","content":"Something that might be useful across all CofD lines (and could work out for some other systems as well) is to leave each player tasked with knowing one sub-system by heart so:<br /> <br /> A is responsible for knowing everything about Mage Sight<br /> B is responsible for knowing all about Crea-Thaum<br /> C is responsible for knowing all about Nimbuses, Summoning and Idk, Social Maneuvering<br /> D gets Chase and Combat rules<br /> <br /> While the ST is supposed to know a bit of everything, having a player specialize in a subset of rules might help speed up things and allow for less book-keeping.<br /> <br /> (Provided the ST doesn't want to wing most stuff, but then, why play a particular system... but this would be an entirely different discussion...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Historian ~ <b><a href=\"http://www.nwodbrasil.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.cronistasdastrevasbr.com</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>I currently ST a... </b>MtAW 2e campaign called &quot;Axis Mundi - Si Vis Pacem&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/250-yuukale","name":"Yuukale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T07:50:05-07:00","id":1150640,"title":"","content":"It's an interesting idea, but I find that it doesn't work at my table. LOL I even forgot about Nimbus mechanics. YAMSS (yet another Mage sub system).<br /> <br /> The down and dirty rules are ok, but starting characters can't do much with it given the low dice pools unless you allow Reach, Yantras etc... in which case it's not that quick and you may as well use the actual spell rules.<br /> <br /> Btw, I know some people think I'm being difficult, but these problem are real with adoption of Mage by the RPG gamer at large.<br /> <br /> Unless we want this game to be restricted to being played by systems analysts with a 140+ IQ, I believe we need to consider pretty heavily how we teach the game to new players.<br /> <br /> For instance, the next group I'm teaching are players aged 12, 15, 17, 29, 47 that have only ever played D&amp;D before.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1470,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","user":"Johnny Awesome","date":". 10-18-2017, 11:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T08:15:14-07:00","id":1150644,"title":"","content":"If you think Nimbus is distracting, keep it purely in the narrative. Ignore NImbus tilts (they only come up for other mages watching you with mage sight while you cast anyway, flaring your nimbus has really disadventageous mechanics) and just use Nimbus as a narrative to give your PCs a feeling for the themes of an NPC when they watch him/her cast something. And long term nimbus of course.... long term nimbus is a delicious story hook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T09:57:26-07:00","id":1150674,"title":"","content":"I will agree that Nimbus Tilts seem overly fiddly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>SWTOR Referal: <a href=\"http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/414-gallus","name":"Gallus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"886"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T16:28:59-07:00","id":1150823,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Johnny Awesome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150640#post1150640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's an interesting idea, but I find that it doesn't work at my table. LOL I even forgot about Nimbus mechanics. YAMSS (yet another Mage sub system).<br /> <br /> The down and dirty rules are ok, but starting characters can't do much with it given the low dice pools unless you allow Reach, Yantras etc... in which case it's not that quick and you may as well use the actual spell rules.<br /> <br /> Btw, I know some people think I'm being difficult, but these problem are real with adoption of Mage by the RPG gamer at large.<br /> <br /> Unless we want this game to be restricted to being played by systems analysts with a 140+ IQ, I believe we need to consider pretty heavily how we teach the game to new players.<br /> <br /> For instance, the next group I'm teaching are players aged 12, 15, 17, 29, 47 that have only ever played D&amp;D before.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I've played with two separate groups (one completely inexperienced with tabletop and another from D&amp;D.) Neither of them read the book. The game with the inexperienced session I discontinued because no one wanted to read anything (this is not a fault of Mage) - because while they were intrigued by the concept, they couldn't get into sifting through the tome to understand their character or the rules of the world (this miiiiiiiiight be the fault of Mage.)<br /> <br /> The &quot;sifting through the tome&quot; is a half-and-half sentiment. I appreciate the level of information and detail in the corebook and supplements, but I also dislike how certain things are written. Sometimes it feels like I need to synchronize with the writer before I can even understand what is being disclosed. While nowhere near as laborious as some of the government or legal documents I regularly parse, they sometimes remind me of that feeling when arguments are presented and you think you understand them but then you need to re-read the document 7 more times to appreciate the nuances so you don't sound like a tit when you try to restate the argument.<br /> <br /> My D&amp;D group is used to being told stuff like: What's your AC? Roll to hit. Roll damage. You can't do that, doesn't really work that way. You can try with disadvantage.<br /> <br /> Essentially, the DM has soundbyte responses to many D&amp;D questions, particularly with regard to combat and actions. Players instantly get used to perception checks, diplomacy, dungeoneering, etc. This is even more evident when you consider the entire &quot;social combat&quot; and &quot;doors&quot; concept of CofD.<br /> <br /> To that end, down&amp;dirty combat is probably easiest to pull off for D&amp;D players because it probably feels as off the cuff as roll 1d20, roll damage. Boom. Whatever. Moving on to the next round or the fight is over and the story continues.<br /> <br /> I want to note that I love Mage (especially 2e) infinitely more than D&amp;D (not to say D&amp;D is bad.) I just think I'm the type of person who would know more about the rules of Mage because I sought out a game like Mage. You essentially need to have inspired, passionate, interested players - or need to motivate them to become that way.<br /> <br /> I, admittedly, do not have everything committed to memory so even I stumble when trying to recall things. I need to get comfortable with tossing rules aside for convenience and dramatic effect instead of trying to recite every rule verbatim. The book tells you to be a little free form if its more fun or convenient. The book even advocates abandoning the rules almost wholesale if it leads to a more fun experience with the product. They made a product and suggest ways we can enjoy it, but ultimately they assure us we ultimately know what is best for us and our groups.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Previous DMs I've had do this all the time when, for whatever reason, they change major characteristics of races or just flat out ignore entire regions of the map or gods. They gloss over what may constitute rolls and checks for expediency, they sometimes extend critical moments to give players time to work out a problem, etc.<br /> <br /> I'd be much more worried about a group of systems analysts with 140+ IQ that want me to follow every rule in the book to the letter - with no freedom to interpret or gloss. That would be a nightmare, and I actually hesitate inviting certain known D&amp;D powergamers into the mix because they do expect their games to play out exactly as the rules they've bothered to read have said.<br /> <br /> After all, to them, why bother reading all the rules if ST can just ignore them whenever?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: Another remedy may be to learn the rules so well that you can reliably coax intent out of players. Mage is so demanding for the ST that I'm thinking of writing up a test for myself to check whether I can recall or approximate without so much as glancing at the book.<br /> <br /> Players use Imagos to tell the ST what they want to do. ST can use their wealth of knowledge to either home in on what that loopy idea would look like in spell factors, practices, arcana - or suggest spells or modifications that are close enough.<br /> <br /> I figure if I can reliably, within 5-10 seconds respond to any spell suggestion with exact parameters or really good &quot;approximate&quot; but exact answers, then I'm golden and the players get to experience the freedom Mage affords. In time they may learn the system or just memorize my antics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":11166,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","user":"kryptonitekrusher","date":". 10-18-2017, 08:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T17:30:53-07:00","id":1150840,"title":"","content":"I feel like Mage is easily the NWoD/ChroD system I've played with the most moving parts. I've probably played nearly a decade of the Storyteller system, playing almost every other gameline and running more than my fair share of mortals, changeling, hunter and demon games. And I have been playing Mage 2E for about a year now bi-weekly and I still feel completely overwhelmed by the system. My wife is also completely incapable of engaging with the mechanics, despite us greatly enjoying the game that we're playing.<br /> <br /> My personal issues with learning the system can probably come down to a few points:<br /> - There's too much jargon that's not mentally easy to differentiate. Why are there so many sub-classes of Yantra? None of us can figure out the various magical tool requirements. Why both praxis and rote? Why use the term rote when rote is already a core NWoD system diceroll mechanic? &quot;Lasting&quot; vs &quot;Persistent&quot;? I can't keep my head around it. And I have a chemistry PhD, which was years of dealing with jargon absolutely everywhere.<br /> - Too many subsystems and they probably could use a severe pruning. Why does the nimbus system exist? Mage sight is my best example here - did it really need three levels? Did it need more mechanics for adding extra sight arcana? Did it need to exist as subsystem compared to Unveiling arcana?<br /> - I never can remember if I need mana or not, I never understand the process of regaining mana outside of pattern scouring which seems straightforward enough, plus I never seemed to understand if you could actually draw mana out of stuff without significant investment in prime, but, that seems to require mana too because not-primary arcana?<br /> - I feel the example spells have made the process of learning the improvised spell system more confusing rather than helpful, because of a whole bunch that do things like &quot;well, this particular spell needs a mana&quot; or &quot;this particular effect seems to need a reach&quot;, it feels like it's added a bunch of complexity to places where the system could have just gone &quot;+1 reach for extra conditional/fine control on any spell&quot; rather than having to reference this one effect and one sub-effect that specifically needs reach.<br /> - There's just a lot to keep track of in general. It's hard not to get caught in decision paralysis.<br /> - The expectation of ritual casting is stifling, it makes it feel like it's hard to react to things as they happen in game and that you have to prepare everything hours beforehand. It can arrest the progress of the game I suppose? We've been having trouble with pacing.<br /> <br /> My wife's had troubles with the system in these sorts of ways:<br /> - Jargon is absolutely killer for her, the linguistic complexity of the terms is extreme even compared to most of the other NWOD.<br /> - The improvisation process for spellcasting is completely lost on her so much so that our ST has basically gone to &quot;work out what you want to try and do and I'll work out the spell for you&quot;, but it results in her never using any improvised spells, basically.<br /> - Her concept is a crafter/magic itemsmith and she still doesn't understand how she's actually supposed to do this...and the rest of the table haven't really worked it out either.<br /> <br /> The table as a whole has been just having problems too:<br /> - No one ever can remember how paradox works or at what points we're supposed to be invoking it and how, even with the quickcasting sheets.<br /> - The system's complexity means that we're basically completely ignoring conditions because there's too much to keep track of. Which of course results in us not getting more than 2-3 beats a session total.<br /> - Generic issue with aspirations/obsessions that unless they're focused very specifically on a group activity of the session we pre-agree to most of us only resolve maybe 1 a session at most, because any personal character aspirations aren't particularly viable unless the player gets a solo spot for a scene or two.<br /> <br /> But those last two aren't really Mage's fault, just a general difficulty with 2E in general.<br /> <br /> I've personally been reading the book, my wife never reads game books because she doesn't learn from them well but it has been a huge struggle compared to all the other WoDs to even get that feeling of being baseline competent with the system and using it to actually accomplish anything. It feels like the complexity of the system is oppressive - I get that once mastery of the system is obtained the system is likely stupidly powerful, but at a Gnosis 4/Arcana 4 mage I feel less powerful and capable as a character than I ever did as a starting Changeling or Demon or Vampire or whatever simply due to how opaque the whole thing feels at times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1462,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","user":"Xenesis","date":". 10-18-2017, 09:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","name":"Xenesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1462_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T19:01:38-07:00","id":1150870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xenesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150840#post1150840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why are there so many sub-classes of Yantra?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's so the rules can cover a variety of different character themes. Do you want to be a shaman with a wooden staff, do you want to tap into your identity as Thor to empower your lightning, do you want to play a necromancer who incorporates your previous victims into your magic, do you use a deck of cards with your magic? <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Lasting&quot; vs &quot;Persistent&quot;? </div> </div> </div>Persistent doesn't have to do with spells. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The expectation of ritual casting is stifling, it makes it feel like it's hard to react to things as they happen in game and that you have to prepare everything hours beforehand. It can arrest the progress of the game I suppose? We've been having trouble with pacing. </div> </div> </div>It is very easy to cast via a single action, it's just a single reach. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Her concept is a crafter/magic itemsmith and she still doesn't understand how she's actually supposed to do this...and the rest of the table haven't really worked it out either. </div> </div> </div>She'll want Prime if she wants to make permanent imbued items and grimoires. But you can make magical items through nearly all arcana by just casting a spell on the object and then trying to get the duration to be a really long time. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No one ever can remember how paradox works or at what points we're supposed to be invoking it and how, even with the quickcasting sheets. </div> </div> </div>Paradox is invoked only really in three scenarios; your reaching past your free amount, your casting obvious magic and muggles see it, or you're casting a spell which you decided to inure after previous wisdom lost.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T19:25:33-07:00","id":1150872,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150870#post1150870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's so the rules can cover a variety of different character themes. Do you want to be a shaman with a wooden staff, do you want to tap into your identity as Thor to empower your lightning, do you want to play a necromancer who incorporates your previous victims into your magic, do you use a deck of cards with your magic?</div> </div> </div> </div>So why can't that be cut down to a very simple &quot;a Yantra is a +1-5 equipment bonus to a spell&quot;? The core ChroD equipment subsystem can essentially be simplified to that level for 99% of use cases. What you've described is just...dressing and that doesn't need a system of its own.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Persistent doesn't have to do with spells. </div> </div> </div>I'm afraid you've lost me. Again, similar jargon all throughout the book and it gets lost on me. I may have been conflating it with Permanent too, now that I think about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is very easy to cast via a single action, it's just a single reach. </div> </div> </div>It is! We finally figured that out after a few weeks of playing. But it's also limiting because it consumes your free reach unless you're using a rote spell. Or have a specific amount more arcana. It's just another checklist item in the big checklist item. Also just as a personal playstyle preference, I never find extended actions in NWoDs interesting in general.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> She'll want Prime if she wants to make permanent imbued items and grimoires. But you can make magical items through nearly all arcana by just casting a spell on the object and then trying to get the duration to be a really long time. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, we never really understood the distinction. And like I said, she cannot grok the spellcasting system at all. Plus a permanent duration with a -10 modifier seems punitive, considering our dicepools never really get into the double digits in the first place? Like, how are you supposed to cast a spell with a modifier like that? This is a very big &quot;how the heck do you do that&quot; that no one in the group really gets, knows how to start with or knows how to distill the process into an understandable and digestible format.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Paradox is invoked only really in three scenarios; your reaching past your free amount, your casting obvious magic and muggles see it, or you're casting a spell which you decided to inure after previous wisdom lost. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but we never remember the particulars of the effects of paradox itself mostly. It took us about 8 months to get the spellcasting flow down so that we'd do the paradox before we'd cast the spell.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1462,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","user":"Xenesis","date":". 10-18-2017, 10:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","name":"Xenesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1462_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T19:47:24-07:00","id":1150874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xenesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150872#post1150872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So why can't that be simplified down to a very simple &quot;a Yantra is a +1-5 equipment bonus to a spell&quot;? The core ChroD equipment subsystem can essentially be simplified to that level for 99% of use cases. What you've described is just...dressing and that doesn't need a system of its own.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it was simplified to that level there would be no reason to customized your character's magic. Every mage would be the same. It'd also mean there would be no reason to use multiple yantras.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm afraid you've lost me. Again, similar jargon all throughout the book and it gets lost on me. </div> </div> </div>Persistent isn't a magic thing. It's a general 2e term for conditions which don't go away normally. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also just as a personal playstyle preference, I never find extended actions in NWoDs interesting in general. </div> </div> </div>It's not an extended action though....<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah, we never really understood the distinction. </div> </div> </div>The distinction is &quot;a spell affects the target&quot; while an imbued item puts an effect on the wearer or user. An imbued item is like &quot;Anyone who wears this hat gets increased strength&quot;, while a spell could make a sword which can strike ghosts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Plus a permanent duration with a -10 modifier seems punitive, considering our dicepools never really get into the double digits in the first place? Like, how are you supposed to cast a spell with a modifier like that? </div> </div> </div>You have to get a lot of yantra and be a skilled mage, most low-level mages wont be able to have their spells be permanent with much regularlity. I more meant like, a spell which lasts a month or two.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T20:06:04-07:00","id":1150878,"title":"","content":"Imbued items and Artifacts could stand better explanation. In one of our games we created an item with a persistent Speak with Beasts effect on the wearer, but it's also supposed to have the Reach effect that allows you to communicate with any animal. There aren't rules for this, so we just added a dot to the Imbued Item Merit. No idea if we did that right.<br /> <br /> The Doors system isn't Mage specific, but it could probably also use clarification. It seems likely that it's intended to be used for social situations that don't require immediate access, so you engage in it when making a requisition from your Order, for instance. This raises the question of why you would engage in multiple scenes to accomplish your objective though when it's possible to simply get past an obstacle with one roll in a single scene in other scenarios. Perhaps Doors are supposed to be used in instances where the ST expects you'll have a longer lasting social relationship with an NPC? Is there a pacing thing to take into consideration that would be a useful guideline?<br /> <br /> Extended Actions, not a fan. I think I'd rather be told that something would take a day or a week to accomplish and then make a single roll. Overall I understand their mechanical niche, but the timeline is decided by the ST and is thus pretty arbitrary making it more difficult to gauge if engaging in a protracted experience is worthwhile.<br /> <br /> None of my problems with Mage stem from the actual spell or attainment mechanics (Other than Imbue Item) of the game though.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11166_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11166\" data-vbnamecard=\"11166\">kryptonitekrusher</a> Good insight on the DnD soundbyte arbitration, I'd never thought of that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 10-18-2017, 11:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T20:07:35-07:00","id":1150879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150874#post1150874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If it was simplified to that level there would be no reason to customized your character's magic. Every mage would be the same. It'd also mean there would be no reason to use multiple yantras.</div> </div> </div> </div>But that seems like...needless extra complexity for what is already likely the most complex subsystem in the NWoD. You've already got the spellcraft in general, why do you need that extra when cutting it down would make it so much more manageable to understand? Our table ignores Yantras half the time during play because it's just another mental tax and calculation when it actually comes down to casting the spells. Even the two experienced players at our table often ignore it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The distinction is &quot;a spell affects the target&quot; while an imbued item puts an effect on the wearer or user. An imbued item is like &quot;Anyone who wears this hat gets increased strength&quot;, while a spell could make a sword which can strike ghosts. </div> </div> </div>... I think that's the first time I've actually understood that concept in relation to the system.<br /> <br /> But like, I'm still not quite sure what you'd actually do with a 'spell affects the target' type thing. Like...what actual effect would you get out of casting say a Space spell or a Matter spell on a fork or something, compared to say just casting that spell directly on something you'd want to affect directly? Like, why would I cast Teleport on a fork and then use it, compared to just casting Teleport on a person? That's where that disconnect lies - none of us (especially my wife and I) can actually...grasp what purpose there is to the vast majority of spellcasting that's not directly on a target.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You have to get a lot of yantra and be a skilled mage, most low-level mages wont be able to have their spells be permanent with much regularlity. I more meant like, a spell which lasts a month or two. </div> </div> </div>Well, like I said above, the Yantra system has been flummoxing us with the bookkeeping so it's been getting ignored a lot. Our average stats are 4 Arcana Max and about 4 Gnosis, so that's pretty damn powerful by most NWoD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","name":"Xenesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1462_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T20:17:03-07:00","id":1150880,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xenesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150879#post1150879\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like, why would I cast Teleport on a fork and then use it, compared to just casting Teleport on a person? That's where that disconnect lies - none of us (especially my wife and I) can actually...grasp what purpose there is to the vast majority of spellcasting that's not directly on a target.</div> </div> </div> </div>The vast majority of the characters who comprise the membership of the various Awakened Sects and Nameless Orders cannot cast spells under their own power, either due to insufficient grasp of the Arcana or simply not being a mage. This is a game whose structure regularly includes apprentices and acolytes and differently-magical entities for whom certain varieties of magical item are game-changers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T20:37:41-07:00","id":1150884,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150880#post1150880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The vast majority of the characters who comprise the membership of the various Awakened Sects and Nameless Orders cannot cast spells under their own power, either due to insufficient grasp of the Arcana or simply not being a mage. This is a game whose structure regularly includes apprentices and acolytes and differently-magical entities for whom certain varieties of magical item are game-changers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose that makes more than enough sense. But more boring and mechanically, like, is it a fork that can teleport or a fork that casts teleport or what? And if it's the latter, how is that controlled? It's a difficulty we're having parsing the system.<br /> <br /> Edit: I suppose this is going to turn into a &quot;What doesn't Xenesis understand about Mage&quot; topic if I keep responding like this, but I hope I've shown where some of the complexity has come from for us. Mage is mechanically complex! I'm struggling! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1462,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","user":"Xenesis","date":". 10-18-2017, 11:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","name":"Xenesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1462_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T22:31:06-07:00","id":1150898,"title":"","content":"It's less &quot;I cast teleport on this fork&quot; because that's just going to teleport the fork. This isn't D&amp;D, there isn't a specific list of effects. Instead it'd be more like &quot;I'll cast a spell which allows this fork to reach the food on this specific plate regardless of the distance between them&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But that seems like...needless extra complexity for what is already likely the most complex subsystem in the NWoD. You've already got the spellcraft in general, why do you need that extra when cutting it down would make it so much more manageable to understand? Our table ignores Yantras half the time during play because it's just another mental tax and calculation when it actually comes down to casting the spells. Even the two experienced players at our table often ignore it. </div> </div> </div>For your group, I'd suggest just using Mantra and Mudra yantra for most spells, and then if you're doing a ritual spell then you can spend some time to flick through the book for a second and see if you have anything viable on hand.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 10-19-2017, 01:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T02:39:04-07:00","id":1150936,"title":"","content":"Yantras are divided into categories because there's a limit to how many dice you can get from a single one. You can exceed this by using multiple categories, which you're gonna need to do for really big spells.<br /> <br /> Also the easiest way to cast a permanent spell is to make the duration the primary factor. Some have that by default and others you need to spend a reach. This means you bump up the duration by arcarnum-1, which if you're arcarnum 4 takes care of 6 dice of those penalties. Now, you can probably manage to cast at 4 dice but a few yantras aren't gonna hurt here, especially if you want to increase other factors.<br /> <br /> If you're casting ritually you can also extend the ritual by it's base time (in the case of gnosis 4, an hour) for an extra die up to 5 times. So in this case a 6 hour ritual would yield the maximum bonus.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T06:37:27-07:00","id":1150978,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1150936#post1150936\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also the easiest way to cast a permanent spell is to make the duration the primary factor. Some have that by default and others you need to spend a reach. This means you bump up the duration by arcarnum-1, which if you're arcarnum 4 takes care of 6 dice of those penalties. Now, you can probably manage to cast at 4 dice but a few yantras aren't gonna hurt here, especially if you want to increase other factors.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>There is a reason <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar14_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"14\" data-vbnamecard=\"14\">Dave Brookshaw</a> says that the mage lexicon is a spread sheet so i can understand peoples confusion of its terms.<br /> No spell can ever be really be called permanent, i think sticking to the proper term &quot;indefinite duration&quot; makes things much easier. Even spells safely released only last till dispelled.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But like, I'm still not quite sure what you'd actually do with a 'spell affects the target' type thing. Like...what actual effect would you get out of casting say a Space spell or a Matter spell on a fork or something, compared to say just casting that spell directly on something you'd want to affect directly? Like, why would I cast Teleport on a fork and then use it, compared to just casting Teleport on a person? That's where that disconnect lies - none of us (especially my wife and I) can actually...grasp what purpose there is to the vast majority of spellcasting that's not directly on a target. </div> </div> </div>In 1e imbued items were either two types: persistent or contingent (ie: always active or needed a trigger). 2e imbued items for the time being seems to have lost these terms and made it hard to mimic persistent effects.<br /> <br /> One can give a imbued ring the ability to cast &quot;honing the form&quot; on activation as per the raw rules but it starts getting muddy if the creator can give the ring a single indefinite honing the form spell with its potency and spell factors already decided so that wearing it always gives the wearer +3 strength. So i can see the confusion when people want to play magical objects smiths or the like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 10-20-2017, 01:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T07:36:43-07:00","id":1150997,"title":"","content":"Yeah - we very deliberately don't say &quot;permanent,&quot; because someone can always dispel it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T07:57:19-07:00","id":1151006,"title":"","content":"Specifically concerning magical items, I imagine that the rules being created in Signs of Sorcery will help to flesh that portion out so people can better grasp how to do what they want to do.<br /> <br /> As to yantras...that's not that terribly complex. You've got a list of several different kinds of yantras that you can use to potentially power your spell. Figuring out which to use is a matter of convenience. Are you casting a buff spell ritually? Maybe trying to go out of your way to find that special location to get a +2 bonus is worth it. Are you casting in the middle of battle? Might want to reconsider using high speech since it's going to force the spell to take longer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7825,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","user":"Falcon777","date":". 10-19-2017, 12:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T10:46:53-07:00","id":1151059,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I think I slipped up and said &quot;permanent&quot; because they kept calling it that in the posts. <br /> <br /> Probably not because I've been reading Exalted stuff a lot lately. Permanent and indefinite mean different things there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-24T07:07:19-07:00","id":1152493,"title":"","content":"In my house rules, I merged reach and spell factors, made the tables more compact and removed default spell factors. Paradox doesn't go up with Gnosis either. So, I removed a lot of mechanical complexity but I kept in all of the setting diversity and expanded on that instead (so I use all the Yantra types and expanded more on them). My system has been popular enough that at least 2 other groups have adopted it and its the official rules for the local LARP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2535-arthexis","name":"arthexis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2535_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-24T07:19:47-07:00","id":1152495,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>arthexis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1149444-system-complexity?p=1152493#post1152493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my house rules, I merged reach and spell factors, made the tables more compact and removed default spell factors. Paradox doesn't go up with Gnosis either. So, I removed a lot of mechanical complexity but I kept in all of the setting diversity and expanded on that instead (so I use all the Yantra types and expanded more on them). My system has been popular enough that at least 2 other groups have adopted it and its the official rules for the local LARP.</div> </div> </div> </div>Would you be willing to share those house rules?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12662-bestial-warlust","name":"bestial Warlust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-25T03:48:55-07:00","id":1152748,"title":"","content":"I'll share them privately with anyone who is interested.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2535-arthexis","name":"arthexis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2535_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}